  "resultText": "`GET /front-desk/queue/metrics` now returns today's average and maximum waiting time, and `npm test` passes (32 unit tests, 5 e2e). `tsc` and `eslint` are also clean.\n\nThe response looks like `{ \"attended\": 2, \"averageWaitingSeconds\": 1200, \"maximumWaitingSeconds\": 1800 }`. If nobody has started an encounter yet today, both values are `null` and `attended` is 0.\n\n**How it works**\n- Front-desk owns visits and clinical owns encounters. To keep the two contexts apart, clinical now emits a new `clinical.encounter-started` event when an encounter starts. Front-desk listens for it and stores the start time on the visit (new `encounterStartedAt` field). The metrics are then computed inside front-desk.\n- Waiting time is encounter start minus check-in.\n- \"Today\" is the UTC day of the app clock, and it counts visits checked in today.\n- Only visitors whose encounter has started are counted. Anyone still waiting is left out.\n\n**Things to know**\n- I added a nullable `encounterStartedAt` column to `FrontDeskVisit` in `prisma/schema.prisma` and re-ran `prisma generate`. Other environments will need `npm run db:push`.\n- Visits whose encounter started before this change have no recorded start time, so they won't count in the metrics.\n- A visitor whose encounter has started still appears in `GET /front-desk/queue`, because that endpoint only looks at check-in status. That was already the case, and I didn't change it.\n\n**Tests**\n- Unit tests in `visit.spec.ts` cover the waiting-time calculation and the average/maximum.\n- A new e2e test in `clinic-flow.e2e-spec.ts` covers the full flow, including a visitor who is still waiting, one who never checked in, and the next day resetting to empty.",
